- op: discuss  # d48c
  at: '2026-09-08T05:53:14Z'  # d48c
  by: agent  # d48c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d48c
  clock: 6  # d48c
  nonce: d48c  # d48c
  message: "THE CONTEXT NUMBER IN THIS ITEM'S SCOPE SECTION IS STALE, AND ITS OWN CAVEAT IS WHY IT MATTERS. The description says \"622 entries / 2,312 names (356 entries with a written rationale)\" at dev 278d1c5fed, and immediately adds \"THE INSTRUMENT RE-TAKES THE COUNT AT RUN TIME; this number is context, not the denominator (a hand-restated closed set drifts).\" It drifted, within one day, exactly as predicted -- so the caveat is now demonstrated rather than merely prudent.\n\nMeasured at dev f4f278b736 (2026-09-08) through the production loader, over the 15 shipped catalogues with no overlays:\n\n    names (primitive records)                      2,312   <- matches to the unit\n    distinct (lang, module, name) keys             2,194\n    \"entries\", counting (boundary, module) groups    563\n    names under an entry carrying `notes`          1,290 (56%)\n\nNAMES reproduces exactly; ENTRIES does not, and there are at least three defensible readings of it (563 / 622 / 773) that disagree on an unchanged catalogue. So \"entry\" is not a unit and this item should report in NAMES.\n\nT1 (the INV-nular re-scope) SHIPPED THE DERIVED COUNT this item anticipated. `verify-claims` now carries `catalog_provenance.kind_adjudication` -- names, names-under-a-written-rationale, and community-overlay names counted apart -- computed from the catalogues each run loaded, in text and in --json. This item's closure condition (\"the census re-run stating N of M names adjudicated and the INV-nular disclosure count updated to it\") therefore has a concrete target: the disclosure recomputes itself, so what this sweep must move is the RATIONALE side, and the closure should be stated as a delta in the derived number rather than as a new literal.\n\nONE CAVEAT INHERITED FROM T1, because it bears on this item's rubric. `notes` is a PROXY: it records that somebody wrote a reason down, not that the row was adjudicated. Nothing records adjudication per row. If this sweep's output is to update \"N of M names adjudicated\" in the disclosure's sense, it needs somewhere to RECORD adjudication per row -- otherwise the count it moves is still the rationale count wearing the adjudication label, which is this family's own failure mode.\n\nALSO RELEVANT TO THE PROBE'S DESIGN: the direction guard that already exists (test_inv_nular_false_sources.py::test_the_direction_sweep_is_otherwise_clean) is NAME-BASED and reaches 598 of 3,405 loaded primitive records (18%). This item's source-reading probe is precisely the complement of that 18%, and the worked example of what falls in the gap is `urllib.request.Request` -- a constructor declared net_send, adjudicated wrong on 2026-08-27, still shipped, and direction-SILENT so the sweep never examined it. Filed separately with its family."  # d48c
- op: discuss  # f29a
  at: '2026-09-11T10:58:02Z'  # f29a
  by: agent  # f29a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f29a
  clock: 7  # f29a
  nonce: f29a  # f29a
  message: "NOT ATTEMPTED 2026-09-11 (arc T5), and the reason is a capability boundary rather than a judgement about the work.\n\nThis item's own method is 'probe-first pool -> PARALLEL SOURCE-CITING SONNET WORKERS with blind plants -> verifier -> candidate list'. Spawning sub-agents is gated in this session: the operating instructions permit it only when the user, a CLAUDE.md file, or a skill asks for it, and this item is none of those -- a tracker item's own method statement does not carry that authority.\n\nWHAT WAS NOT DONE INSTEAD, so the gap is not mistaken for a finding: no single-threaded substitute was run. A serial pass over the same pool would not be the same measurement -- the design's blind plants exist to calibrate workers against each other, and one worker cannot be blind to itself. Running it serially and reporting the output as this item's sweep would be the 'instrument that does not model the thing under test' failure in a new costume.\n\nTO UNBLOCK: an explicit instruction to use sub-agents for this sweep, or a decision to re-scope the method to something a single agent can execute honestly."  # f29a
- op: discuss  # bcbb
  at: '2026-09-11T16:22:11Z'  # bcbb
  by: agent  # bcbb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bcbb
  clock: 8  # bcbb
  nonce: bcbb  # bcbb
  message: "FIRST TRANCHE RUN 2026-09-11, owner authorised 3 parallel workers. Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vafad_t5_09112026/ (PLAN.md pre-registered with the scoring rubric before any verdict existed; RESULT.md; candidates.json).\n\nSCOPE COVERED, stated before the findings. Catalogue RE-COUNTED at run time as this item requires -- its filed figure of 622 entries / 2,312 names is stale: 3,505 rows across 15 languages, 2,018 carrying a written rationale, REMAINDER 1,487 (42.4%). Probed this session: the 335 on-disk rows (python 253, rust 82). NOT probed and DECLARED: 981 rows in the eight raw.githubusercontent languages, 98 objc/swift rows REFUSED because developer.apple.com is off the allowlist, 73 bash/c/cpp rows with no git-hosted stdlib. THIS TRANCHE IS 22.5% OF THE REMAINDER.\n\nLAYER 1, mechanical, no model. python CONSISTENT 88 / MISMATCH 15 / NO_SIGNAL 62 / NATIVE 88. rust CONSISTENT 48 / MISMATCH 25 / NO_SIGNAL 4 / NATIVE 5. POOL = 106, REFUSED = 93.\n\nLAYER 3 PLANTS. This item names the historical wrong rows as plants (haskell IORef, c socket, javascript path.*, erlang io_lib:format). NONE are python or rust, and a foreign-language row dropped into a python chunk is NOT BLIND -- a worker spots the intruder and the control measures nothing. Plants were therefore built the way those rows were WRONG: an adjudicated rationale-carrying row with its boundary flipped to a different medium. 9 MISLABEL + 9 CORRECT.\n\nSCORING, against the rubric fixed in advance. chunk1 42 rows / plants 3 of 4 / false-flags 2 of 3 / 0 bad citations. chunk2 41 / 1 of 2 / 0 of 3 / 0. chunk3 41 / 3 of 3 / 0 of 3 / 0. ALL THREE PASS. Both \"missed\" MISLABEL plants are C-implemented builtins the worker correctly reported UNREADABLE -- UNCATCHABLE BY CONSTRUCTION, a fault in my plant selection rather than worker recall. Against catchable plants the workers went 7 FOR 7. All 124 citations resolve to real files on disk.\n\nTHREE INSTRUMENT FAULTS, ALL MINE, ALL CAUGHT BEFORE THEY REACHED A NUMBER. (1) The first rust probe located a function by BASE NAME across all of rust-src and concatenated up to four bodies, so std::fs::read came back carrying db+fs+net+proc signals; 37 of 82 rows contaminated and every error ran FALSE-ALL-CLEAR. Rebuilt to resolve the module path to a file first and refuse when it cannot. (2) The scorer counted UNREADABLE as a disagreement, so honest refusals scored as false flags -- this alone flipped chunk1 from DOWNGRADE to PASS. (3) The citation check did not expand \"~\", calling 13 of 41 valid rust-src citations unusable.\n\n25 CANDIDATES, cited, disagreeing. NOT EDITS (rubric R4). Four verified by me against the same sources:\n\n(a) ssl.SSLObject.read / .write / .do_handshake, declared net_recv / net_send -- THE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL. The class docstring at ssl.py:784-797 states it \"does not provide any network IO itself\" and explicitly \"lacks ... Any form of network IO, including methods such as recv and send\"; IO goes through memory BIOs. THESE THREE ROWS MINT untrusted_input AT FUNCTIONS THAT PERFORM NO NETWORK I/O -- a taint source from nowhere, i.e. a false-positive generator.\n\n(b) std::process::exit / ::abort, declared subprocess. Verified: abort() is one line calling crate::sys::abort_internal(), terminating the CALLING process. Neither spawns nor communicates with a subprocess.\n\n(c) platform.architecture, declared host_info_read, ACTUALLY SPAWNS A SUBPROCESS -- platform.py:720 calls _syscmd_file(executable, ...), documented as \"Interface to the system's file command\", which runs subprocess.check_output([\"file\",\"-b\",...]). A MISSED boundary, not a cosmetic one.\n\n(d) std::env::current_dir, declared fs_read -- A PARITY BREAK, 3 AGAINST 1: python os.getcwd, go os.Getwd and c unistd.getcwd are all host_info_read. It also disagrees with its OWN module neighbours current_exe / temp_dir / home_dir, all host_info_read.\n\nSIX ROWS LAND ON UNDECIDABLE_BY_ARGUMENT -- asyncio.StreamReader.read and .readuntil (one generic class backs both network and subprocess transports), ElementTree.parse, importlib.import_module, importlib.resources.files, platform.libc_ver. That is INV-zumin dual-row shape surfacing in the remainder: a structural result, not a per-row one.\n\nNULL TEST FROM THE PRE-REGISTRATION: a disagreement counts only if the cited MECHANISM contradicts the declared boundary, not merely if the probe bucketed it differently. All 25 survive -- each carries a read mechanism. THIS TRANCHE IS NOT A NULL RESULT.\n\nNEXT, per R4: no catalogue row edited here. Each accepted candidate becomes its own fix item paying ADR-0049 ruling 3 separately."  # bcbb
- op: discuss  # da93
  at: '2026-09-13T06:23:45Z'  # da93
  by: agent  # da93
  actor: jgstern_agent  # da93
  clock: 9  # da93
  nonce: da93  # da93
  message: "TRANCHE 2 RUN AND SCORED 2026-09-13. Owner authorised 3 parallel workers as STANDING for the rest of this sweep, and approved adding developer.apple.com to the allowlist (PR #940), which returned the 98 objc/swift rows tranche 1 refused. Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/vafad_t5_t2_09132026/ (PLAN-T2.md pre-registered before any worker ran, five appended addenda with originals untouched; OBSERVATIONS.md carries every correction; RESULT-T2.md).\n\nCOVERAGE. Tranche 1 took the 335 on-disk rows. This tranche took the 981 in the eight git-hosted languages PLUS the 98 objc/swift. CUMULATIVE COVERAGE OF THE REMAINDER IS NOW 95.1% (1,414 of 1,487). The residual 73 are bash/c/cpp rows with no git-hosted stdlib, declared rather than silently omitted.\n\nTHE 981 ARE ONLY 591 DISTINCT UNITS, which is what made this affordable. Every one of erlang's 178 remainder rows is an OTP function elixir also rows; every one of kotlin's 105 is a JDK method java and scala also row. A unit was adjudicated ONCE and the verdict fanned back to its rows. New rubric rule R5 forces units AND rows to be reported together so a candidate count is never misread as a row count.\n\nSCALE. 17 worker runs (13 chunks + 4 gap re-runs), 464 adjudications: 381 agree (82.1%), 75 disagree (16.2%), 8 unreadable (1.7%). 396 citations checked by opening every file: ZERO missing, ZERO out of range. Plants: 39 MISLABEL to 37 caught (94.9%); 39 CORRECT to 33 correctly left, 3 false-flagged.\n\nCANDIDATES: 30 UNITS / 39 ROWS, 7.8% of 386 reviewed. Three exclusion mechanisms ran and ALL THREE REMOVED WORK: 2 contested (go1 and go2 adjudicated the generic-io class in opposite directions, both citing source correctly, so it is held as a design question rather than settled by worker vote), 3 refuted by standing rulings, 1 R3 discard resolved by repairing the parser.\n\nTHE RESULT THAT MATTERS MOST: 70% OF CANDIDATES PROPOSE REMOVAL (21 units / 27 rows) rather than re-pointing (9 units / 12 rows). Removing a row REDUCES detection, which is the false-all-clear direction. Every one of the 21 must pay ADR-0049 ruling 3's direction guard, and never-pin-a-removal binds all of them. A sweep whose main product is \"delete these rows\" deserves more suspicion than one that re-points them, and it is stated that way rather than presented as 30 clean wins.\n\nTHE DEFECTS ARE ROWS, NOT UNITS. System.Info host_info_read [os, arch, compilerName, compilerVersion] -- all four fixed at GHC BUILD time, VERIFIED BY ME IN SOURCE (os = HOST_OS at Info.hs:74, arch = HOST_ARCH at :107, compilerName = the literal \"ghc\" at :113). host_info_read makes a row a TAINT SOURCE, so this mints a source structurally unable to carry runtime host data. System.Exit env_write [exitWith, exitFailure, exitSuccess, die] -- process termination, and env_ means may-carry-a-credential, which an exit code does not. The erlang application env_write row -- set_env/unset_env are CORRECT under the credential ruling, the other five are lifecycle operations, which is the WI-jupaf module-sweep defect recurring two lines below where it was already fixed. System.Directory fs_read -- getHomeDirectory and getTemporaryDirectory read HOME and TMPDIR, not the filesystem, confirmed by three independent workers. os/exec subprocess -- LookPath and CommandContext never create a process.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT: A NEW DEFECT CLASS, and I was one write-up away from publishing it as one. io_boundary.py:2239 -- the docstring of the function that computes INV-nular's own count -- records that SEVEN such families were already swept out of the vouched rows, naming \"constructors declared net_send\" explicitly. The honest result is more useful: those families are STILL PRESENT IN THE REMAINDER, in five languages the original sweep never reached. That is evidence about the remainder's COMPOSITION, which is what this item exists to measure and what the 2026-09-06 disclosed-rather-than-read ruling was taken without.\n\nPRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS: 1 HELD, 2 REFUTED. P1 held (7.8% against tranche 1's 23.6%). P2 refuted narrowly (javascript fewest at 0.0%, not jvm). P3 REFUTED BADLY and is the useful one: I predicted BEAM would yield the most because OTP's process/messaging surface is where boundaries blur, naming gen_event, gen_statem, proc_lib, global. Those modules almost all SURVIVED; beam came second-lowest at 5.4% and haskell led at 12.7%. I ranked languages by how AMBIGUOUS their I/O is; the rate is actually driven by WHAT KIND OF THING the remainder contains -- constants and control-flow primitives generate defects, while genuinely ambiguous I/O got rowed correctly because whoever rowed it had to stop and think.\n\nSIX INSTRUMENT FAULTS, ALL MINE, TWO IN THE FLATTERING DIRECTION. One-hop delegation following was not enough (stdlibs are thin wrappers, three hops deep). Interface declarations read as NO_SIGNAL. Re-export shells broke haskell. Java interfaces carry no access modifier. FLATTERING #1: check_rulings.py matched \"module: X\" against lines reading \"- module: X\", so EVERY candidate came back unruled -- caught only because the bucket came back empty. FLATTERING #2: the R3 parser rejected line RANGES; repairing it ADMITTED a candidate, so it was measured first (15 of 320 citations use ranges, systematic), the threshold left untouched, and the direction disclosed.\n\nPLUS TWO PLANT-CONSTRUCTION FAULTS THE SCORE CANNOT SHOW. Plants built from packages I never vendored are UNCATCHABLE BY CONSTRUCTION -- tranche 1 made this exact error and I repeated it. Remedy was to vendor the packages and re-run all 61 affected units, not to excuse the misses. And a plant flipped FROM AN ALREADY-WRONG VALUE tests nothing, which produced the best finding of the tranche.\n\nTHAT FINDING: apple1 \"missed\" the Process.terminate plant, flipped from subprocess to process_send; the worker agreed with process_send citing kill(pid, SIGTERM). The row it was flipped FROM is out of parity. python and rust file process signalling under ipc_send with a DATED 2026-09-06 WI-dupok ruling (\"signals the child; start() is the launch and sits under subprocess\"); objc and swift file terminate/interrupt under subprocess with NO ruling, and objc's note claims equivalence to python's subprocess.run, which is precisely where python SPLITS. Three rows against two. These are VOUCHED rows from plant stock, so NOT a tranche candidate -- recorded in the flagged-vouched-row stream with bufio.NewScanner, bufio.Reader.ReadLine and NotificationCenter.post.\n\nNEW RUBRIC RULE R6, WHICH SHOULD OUTLIVE THIS TRANCHE. A worker disagreement is NOT a candidate until it has been checked against the catalogue's own YAML notes and ADRs. Workers are deliberately blind to those -- one who could read a row's rationale would rationalise the row -- so the check belongs to the orchestrator. Mechanised as check_rulings.py, which also flags when a module has ALREADY BEEN SPLIT across boundaries, because a proposal to move one member then re-opens a completed sort. It refuted three candidates here, ALL THREE in the safety-relevant direction: each would have moved a generic accessor that can read a keystore password out of the credential-bearing bucket.\n\nAPPLE CAVEAT, which travels with every apple row: swift-corelibs-foundation is a REIMPLEMENTATION for non-Darwin platforms, not Apple's shipping binary. Those citations evidence MECHANISM; developer.apple.com is authoritative for CONTRACT. Apple also had NO mechanical layer-1 triage, because selector-to-Swift-symbol mapping is a naming convention a regex gets wrong silently, so all 98 went to workers while other families sent only pooled units. Its 12.2% rate must be read with both facts.\n\nNO CATALOGUE ROW WAS EDITED (R4). Next step is filing the ROW-LEVEL families as fix items, each paying ADR-0049 ruling 3 separately."  # da93
